ecision_sf))\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<div id=\"htmlwidget-c702dd27fded759e1f68\" style=\"width:100%;height:480px;\" class=\"maplibregl html-widget\"></div>\n<script type=\"application/json\" data-for=\"htmlwidget-c702dd27fded759e1f68\">{\"x\":{\"style\":\"https://basemaps.cartocdn.com/gl/positron-gl-style/style.json\",\"center\":[0,0],\"zoom\":0,\"bearing\":0,\"pitch\":0,\"projection\":\"globe\",\"additional_params\":{\"bounds\":[-74,-52,-54,-22]},\"setProjection\":[{\"projection\":\"globe\"}],\"layers\":[{\"id\":\"quickview\",\"type\":\"fill\",\"source\":{\"type\":\"geojson\",\"data\":{\"type\":\"FeatureCollection\",\"features\":[{\"type\":\"Feature\",\"properties\":{\"OGC_FID\":0.0,\"CNTR_ID\":\"AR\",\"NAME_ENGL\":\"Argentina\",\"ISO3_CODE\":\"ARG\",\"CNTR_NAME\":\"Argentina\",\"FID\":\"AR\",\"date\":\"2021-01-01\",\"popup_content\":\"<strong>OGC_FID:\\u003c/strong> 0<br><strong>CNTR_ID:\\u003c/strong> AR<br><strong>NAME_ENGL:\\u003c/strong> Argentina<br><strong>ISO3_CODE:\\u003c/strong> ARG<br><strong>CNTR_NAME:\\u003c/strong> Argentina<br><strong>FID:\\u003c/strong> AR<br><strong>date:\\u003c/strong> 18628\"},\"geometry\":{\"type\":\"MultiPolygon\",\"coordinates\":[[[[-62.0,-23.0],[-61.0,-23.0],[-61.0,-24.0],[-60.0,-24.0],[-59.0,-25.0],[-58.0,-25.0],[-58.0,-26.0],[-58.0,-27.0],[-57.0,-27.0],[-56.0,-28.0],[-56.0,-29.0],[-57.0,-30.0],[-58.0,-30.0],[-58.0,-31.0],[-58.0,-32.0],[-58.0,-33.0],[-59.0,-34.0],[-58.0,-34.0],[-58.0,-35.0],[-57.0,-35.0],[-57.0,-36.0],[-57.0,-37.0],[-57.0,-38.0],[-58.0,-38.0],[-59.0,-39.0],[-61.0,-39.0],[-62.0,-39.0],[-62.0,-40.0],[-62.0,-41.0],[-63.0,-41.0],[-64.0,-41.0],[-65.0,-41.0],[-65.0,-42.0],[-64.0,-42.0],[-64.0,-43.0],[-65.0,-43.0],[-65.0,-44.0],[-65.0,-45.0],[-66.0,-45.0],[-67.0,-45.0],[-67.0,-46.0],[-67.0,-47.0],[-66.0,-47.0],[-66.0,-48.0],[-67.0,-49.0],[-68.0,-49.0],[-68.0,-50.0],[-69.0,-50.0],[-69.0,-51.0],[-69.0,-52.0],[-70.0,-52.0],[-72.0,-52.0],[-72.0,-51.0],[-73.0,-51.0],[-73.0,-50.0],[-74.0,-50.0],[-73.0,-49.0],[-72.0,-48.0],[-72.0,-47.0],[-72.0,-46.0],[-72.0,-45.0],[-71.0,-45.0],[-71.0,-44.0],[-72.0,-44.0],[-72.0,-43.0],[-72.0,-42.0],[-72.0,-41.0],[-72.0,-40.0],[-71.0,-39.0],[-71.0,-38.0],[-71.0,-37.0],[-71.0,-36.0],[-70.0,-36.0],[-70.0,-35.0],[-70.0,-34.0],[-70.0,-33.0],[-70.0,-32.0],[-71.0,-32.0],[-71.0,-31.0],[-70.0,-31.0],[-70.0,-30.0],[-70.0,-29.0],[-70.0,-28.0],[-69.0,-28.0],[-69.0,-27.0],[-69.0,-26.0],[-69.0,-25.0],[-68.0,-25.0],[-68.0,-24.0],[-67.0,-24.0],[-67.0,-23.0],[-67.0,-22.0],[-66.0,-22.0],[-65.0,-22.0],[-64.0,-22.0],[-63.0,-22.0],[-62.0,-23.0]]],[[[-56.0,-27.0],[-55.0,-27.0],[-55.0,-28.0],[-56.0,-28.0],[-56.0,-27.0]]],[[[-55.0,-26.0],[-54.0,-26.0],[-54.0,-27.0],[-55.0,-27.0],[-55.0,-26.0]]]]}}]},\"generateId\":true},\"source_layer\":null,\"paint\":{\"fill-antialias\":true,\"fill-color\":\"navy\",\"fill-opacity\":0.6,\"fill-outline-color\":\"white\",\"fill-translate-anchor\":\"map\"},\"layout\":{\"visibility\":\"visible\"},\"slot\":null,\"minzoom\":null,\"maxzoom\":null,\"popup\":\"popup_content\",\"tooltip\":null,\"tooltip_style\":null,\"popup_style\":null,\"hover_options\":null,\"before_id\":null,\"filter\":null}]},\"evals\":[],\"jsHooks\":[]}</script>\n```\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n## extent helpers\n\nA full set of extent helpers landed too: `ddbs_xmax()`, `ddbs_xmin()`, `ddbs_ymax()`, `ddbs_ymin()`, plus the Z/M equivalents. By default they work `by_feature = TRUE` adding a new column per feature (useful for maintaining workflows within duckdb, and apply filters):\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nargentina_ddbs |> \n  select(NAME_ENGL) |> \n  ddbs_xmin() |> \n  ddbs_xmax()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n\n```\n# A duckspatial lazy spatial table\n# ● CRS: EPSG:4326 \n# ● Geometry column: geom \n# ● Geometry type: POLYGON \n# ● Bounding box: xmin: -73.525 ymin: -52.398 xmax: -53.624 ymax: -21.818 \n# Data backed by DuckDB (dbplyr lazy evaluation)\n# Use ddbs_collect() or st_as_sf() to materialize to sf\n#\n# Source:   table<temp_table_3c4a40a8_b6f4_48d1_9096_fc118e38818f> [?? x 4]\n# Database: DuckDB 1.5.4 [cidre@Linux 7.0.0-22-generic:R 4.6.0/:memory:]\n  NAME_ENGL  xmin  xmax geom                                                    \n  <chr>     <dbl> <dbl> <wk_wkb>                                                \n1 Argentina -73.5 -53.6 <POLYGON ((-62.6452 -22.25047, -62.25779 -22.53346, -61…\n```\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\nHowever, we can get the global minimum or maximum:\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nddbs_xmax(argentina_ddbs, by_feature = FALSE)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n\n```\n[1] -53.62409\n```\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n## ddbs_dimension\n\nAnd `ddbs_dimension()` returns the topological dimension of each geometry (0 = point, 1 = line, 2 = polygon):\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nddbs_dimension(argentina_ddbs)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n\n```\n# A duckspatial lazy spatial table\n# ● CRS: EPSG:4326 \n# ● Geometry column: geom \n# ● Geometry type: POLYGON \n# ● Bounding box: xmin: -73.525 ymin: -52.398 xmax: -53.624 ymax: -21.818 \n# Data backed by DuckDB (dbplyr lazy evaluation)\n# Use ddbs_collect() or st_as_sf() to materialize to sf\n#\n# Source:   table<temp_table_aa7a5276_5b15_4455_80e2_a86c17310ae3> [?? x 9]\n# Database: DuckDB 1.5.4 [cidre@Linux 7.0.0-22-generic:R 4.6.0/:memory:]\n  OGC_FID CNTR_ID NAME_ENGL ISO3_CODE CNTR_NAME FID   date       dimension geom \n    <dbl> <chr>   <chr>     <chr>     <chr>     <chr> <date>         <int> <wk_>\n1       0 AR      Argentina ARG       Argentina AR    2021-01-01         2 <POL…\n```\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\nRounding out the polygon-side additions, `ddbs_get_ninterior_rings()` counts holes in a POLYGON.\n\n:::\n\n## Points, lines, and measurements\n\n`ddbs_point()` creates POINT geometries directly from coordinate vectors, with 2D/3D/4D support and CRS assignment:\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\ncities_ddbs <- ddbs_point(\n  x = c(-58.3816, -64.1811),\n  y = c(-34.6037, -31.4201),\n  another_column = c(\"A\", \"B\"),\n  crs = 4326\n)\n\ncities_ddbs\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n\n```\n# A duckspatial lazy spatial table\n# ● CRS: EPSG:4326 \n# ● Geometry column: geometry \n# ● Geometry type: POINT \n# ● Bounding box: xmin: -64.181 ymin: -34.604 xmax: -58.382 ymax: -31.42 \n# Data backed by DuckDB (dbplyr lazy evaluation)\n# Use ddbs_collect() or st_as_sf() to materialize to sf\n#\n# Source:   table<temp_table_a7614852_e444_4ae3_adf4_77eb75c593ae> [?? x 2]\n# Database: DuckDB 1.5.4 [cidre@Linux 7.0.0-22-generic:R 4.6.0/:memory:]\n  another_column geometry                   \n  <chr>          <wk_wkb>                   \n1 A              <POINT (-58.3816 -34.6037)>\n2 B              <POINT (-64.1811 -31.4201)>\n```\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\nFor line work, `ddbs_shortest_line()` returns the connecting LINESTRING between the closest points of two geometries, and `ddbs_line_locate_point()` gives the fractional position (0–1) of the closest point on a line to a reference point. `ddbs_line_node()` is the batch version: it nodes a set of lines, splitting them at every crossing into a fully noded MULTILINESTRING.\n\n`ddbs_azimuth()` computes the clockwise bearing between two sets of points, in radians by default or degrees via `unit = \"degrees\"`.\n\nOn the aggregation side, `ddbs_intersection_agg()` joins `ddbs_union_agg()` as the intersection counterpart — computes the common area of a set of geometries, optionally grouped by column.\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\n## create three overlapping polygons\npolys <- sf::st_as_sf(\n  data.frame(grp = c(\"a\", \"a\", \"b\", \"b\")),\n  geometry = sf::st_sfc(\n    sf::st_polygon(list(matrix(c(0,0, 3,0, 3,3, 0,3, 0,0), ncol = 2, byrow = TRUE))),\n    sf::st_polygon(list(matrix(c(1,1, 4,1, 4,4, 1,4, 1,1), ncol = 2, byrow = TRUE))),\n    sf::st_polygon(list(matrix(c(2,2, 5,2, 5,5, 2,5, 2,2), ncol = 2, byrow = TRUE))),\n    sf::st_polygon(list(matrix(c(3,3, 6,3, 6,6, 3,6, 3,3), ncol = 2, byrow = TRUE)))\n  ),\n  crs = 4326\n)\n\n## intersect all geometries into their common area\npolys_inters <- ddbs_intersection_agg(polys, mode = \"sf\")\n\n## intersect within groups\npolys_inters_grp <- ddbs_intersection_agg(polys, by = \"grp\", mode = \"sf\")\n\n## plot them\nggplot() +\n  geom_sf(data = polys, aes(fill = grp), alpha = 0.3) +\n  geom_sf(data = polys_inters_grp, aes(fill = grp), alpha = 0.95) +\n  geom_sf(data = polys_inters, size = 4) +\n  geom_text(aes(x = 3, y = 3, label = \"(3,3)\"), nudge_x = 0.2, nudge_y = 0.1) +\n  scale_x_continuous(breaks = 0:6) +\n  scale_y_continuous(breaks = 0:6) +\n  theme_minimal() +\n  labs(fill = \"grp\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![](index_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-9-1.png){width=672}\n:::\n:::\n\n\nNote that we have generated:\n\n* `polys_inters` (the black point): intersects all four polygons together (ignoring `grp`). Since each square is shifted diagonally by 1 unit and only 3×3 in size, the only area common to all four is that single point at (3,3).\n\n* `polys_inters_grp`: the intersection computed **within each group** (`by = \"grp\"`). The two transparent red squares (`grp = \"a\"`) overlap in the red square; the two transparent teal squares (`grp = \"b\"`) overlap in the teal square.\n\n## Serialization round-trips\n\n`ddbs_as_geojson()`, `ddbs_as_wkb()`, and friends already let you serialize geometries out. Now there's a matching set of parsers to bring them back in: `ddbs_geom_from_text()`, `ddbs_geom_from_wkb()`, `ddbs_geom_from_hexwkb()`, `ddbs_geom_from_hexewkb()`, and `ddbs_geom_from_geojson()`.\n\n`ddbs_as_geojson()` itself got more useful: it now carries all non-geometry columns as feature `properties` instead of dropping them, and returns a single `FeatureCollection` by default — matching `geojsonsf::sf_geojson()`. Pass `feature_collection = FALSE` if you want one `Feature` per row instead.\n\n## Vector tiles\n\nTwo new functions bring Mapbox Vector Tile support into {duckspatial}: `ddbs_as_mvt_geom()` transforms geometries into MVT coordinate space, clipping to a tile's bounding box. `ddbs_write_mbtiles()` goes further and generates a full tile pyramid from a spatial dataset, writing it straight to an MBTiles file — ready to serve or convert to PMTiles.\n\n## Extension management\n\n`ddbs_extension_info()` prints a `glimpse()` of a DuckDB extension's row from `duckdb_extensions()` — installed/loaded status, version, install path — for the spatial extension by default:\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nconn <- ddbs_create_conn()\nddbs_extension_info(conn)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n\n```\nRows: 1\nColumns: 9\n$ extension_name    <chr> \"spatial\"\n$ loaded            <lgl> TRUE\n$ installed         <lgl> TRUE\n$ install_path      <chr> \"/home/cidre/R/x86_64-pc-linux-gnu-library/4.6/duckd…\n$ description       <chr> \"Geospatial extension that adds support for working …\n$ aliases           <list> <>\n$ extension_version <chr> \"28db190\"\n$ install_mode      <chr> \"REPOSITORY\"\n$ installed_from    <chr> \"core\"\n```\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThis is not restricted to `spatial`, you can use it with any other extensio:\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nddbs_extension_info(conn, \"h3\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n\n```\nRows: 1\nColumns: 9\n$ extension_name    <chr> \"h3\"\n$ loaded            <lgl> FALSE\n$ installed         <lgl> TRUE\n$ install_path      <chr> \"/home/cidre/R/x86_64-pc-linux-gnu-library/4.6/duckd…\n$ description       <chr> \"\"\n$ aliases           <list> <>\n$ extension_version <chr> \"be9ee91\"\n$ install_mode      <chr> \"REPOSITORY\"\n$ installed_from    <chr> \"community\"\n```\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n`ddbs_install()` gains a `repos` argument to pull an extension from a specific DuckDB repository (`\"core\"`, `\"core_nightly\"`, `\"community\"`). Leaving it `NULL` keeps the previous fallback behavior (core, then community).\n\n## Bug fixes\n\n* `ddbs_install()` had a broken \"already on the latest version\" check — it referenced a column DuckDB doesn't provide and compared `install_mode` with the wrong case, so it silently never triggered. Removed.\n* A mistake in the startup message is fixed.\n* `ddbs_union_agg()` gains a `mem` argument: set `mem = TRUE` to use `ST_MemUnion_Agg()` instead of `ST_Union_Agg()` — slower, but lighter on memory for large unions.\n\n## Wrapping up\n\nThat's about 20 new functions across the two releases, plus some quality-of-life fixes for extension installation and GeoJSON export. As always, full changelogs are on [GitHub](https://github.com/Cidree/duckspatial/releases), and the package is on [CRAN](https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=duckspatial).\n\nFeedback and issues are very welcome — [open one here](https://github.com/Cidree/duckspatial/issues) if you run into anything.\n\n## Session Information\n\nAnalyses were conducted using the R Statistical language (version 4.6.0; R Core\nTeam, 2026) on Ubuntu 26.04 LTS, using the packages duckspatial (version 1.2.0;\nCidre González A et al., 2026), sf (version 1.1.1; Pebesma E, Bivand R, 2023),\nmapgl (version 0.5.0; Walker K, 2026), ggplot2 (version 4.0.3; Wickham H, 2016)\nand dplyr (version 1.2.1.9000; Wickham H et al., 2026).\n\nReferences\n----------\n  - Cidre González A, Kotov E, Pereira R (2026). _duckspatial: R Interface to\n'DuckDB' Database with Spatial Extension_.\ndoi:10.32614/CRAN.package.duckspatial\n<https://doi.org/10.32614/CRAN.package.duckspatial>. R package version 1.2.0,\n<https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=duckspatial>.\n  - Pebesma E, Bivand R (2023). _Spatial Data Science: With applications in R_.\nChapman and Hall/CRC. doi:10.1201/9780429459016\n<https://doi.org/10.1201/9780429459016>. <https://r-spatial.org/book/>. Pebesma\nE (2018). \"Simple Features for R: Standardized Support for Spatial Vector\nData.\" _The R Journal_, *10*(1), 439-446. doi:10.32614/RJ-2018-009\n<https://doi.org/10.32614/RJ-2018-009>. <https://doi.org/10.32614/RJ-2018-009>.\n  - R Core Team (2026). _R: A Language and Environment for Statistical\nComputing_. R Foundation for Statistical Computing, Vienna, Austria.\ndoi:10.32614/R.manuals <https://doi.org/10.32614/R.manuals>.\n<https://www.R-project.org/>.\n  - Walker K (2026). _mapgl: Interactive Maps with 'Mapbox GL JS' and 'MapLibre\nGL JS'_. doi:10.32614/CRAN.package.mapgl\n<https://doi.org/10.32614/CRAN.package.mapgl>. R package version 0.5.0,\n<https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=mapgl>.\n  - Wickham H (2016). _ggplot2: Elegant Graphics for Data Analysis_.\nSpringer-Verlag New York. ISBN 978-3-319-24277-4.\n<https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org>.\n  - Wickham H, François R, Henry L, Müller K, Vaughan D (2026). _dplyr: A Grammar\nof Data Manipulation_. R package version 1.2.1.9000, commit\nd5e94e7fa8fd4a5f79c1a707d1842216bb4c691f, <https://github.com/tidyverse/dplyr>.",
